WAMR's AOT loader: the target symbol maps const (our local patch 0004, for decision 68)

Applies to: WAMR main ea5dd88 (github.com/wasm-micro-runtime/wasm-micro-runtime) with #5146, #5148 and the
os_self_thread patch, the pinned series of docs/wasm-integration.md; the result is tree 690282c7. A
modification of WAMR, so under Apache-2.0 WITH LLVM-exception, NOT this repository's MIT.

Why: with STU_WASM on, the stand-in stopped at boot with "standin: out of memory" (S3, 2026-10-09). keys_img
needs 96,000 B of contiguous internal RAM, and the off image's largest block there is 98,304 (heap_caps,
which reports in 4,096 B steps). Most of WAMR's internal DRAM was two writable tables: target_sym_map (1,000 B,
the Xtensa symbol map) and quick_aot_entries (768 B). Boots, largest internal block before keys_img:
off 98,304 (boots); on 94,208 (fails); on with WAMR_BUILD_QUICK_AOT_ENTRY=0 94,208 (fails); on with that and
this patch 98,304 (boots, and the S3 gate script passes; #5500).

Audit: get_target_symbol_map() has two callers, both in aot_loader.c (get_native_symbol_by_name and
resolve_target_sym), and both only read symbol_name and symbol_addr. x86_64's init_plt_table() copies
symbol_addr into its PLT. Nothing else names target_sym_map. Declared const, any write would not compile.
quick_aot_entries is qsorted at init, so it cannot be const; the firmware turns it off instead
(WAMR_BUILD_QUICK_AOT_ENTRY=0).

Measured, with this patch and quick entries off: the stand-in's static D/IRAM with the option on goes from
219,343 + 2,100 to 219,343 + 324 B (off is 219,343);
target_sym_map is at 0x3c120688 (flash rodata). Built for xtensa (the firmware) and x86_64 (oracle/wasm_check.sh,
green); the other architectures' files were changed the same way and not built.

From ce4b135434e9c7b3dfb9bac62a0bfec87c12edf5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Michael Stufflebeam <cpuchip@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 9 Oct 2026 16:26:33 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] aot: keep the target symbol maps const

Nothing writes target_sym_map: the loader only looks symbols up in it,
and x86_64 copies addresses out of it into its PLT. Declared const, it
goes to flash rodata on a microcontroller instead of internal RAM. On the
ESP32-S3 that is 1,000 bytes of DRAM for the Xtensa map.

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
